							</td>
							<td width="75%" align="left">
								&nbsp;<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php#1">General Category</a> / <a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?board=2.0">Non-EQ (Hey Real Life!)</a> / <a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1273.15#msg12694">Re: Sibling Punishment. . .?</a>
							</td>
							<td align="right" style="padding: 0 1ex; white-space: nowrap;">
								on: November 16, 2004, 12:08:36 pm
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left">I should come out of my box?  Foster parenting has taught me a lot Moraganna and one of the first things I suggest NO COMMAND is that communication be established between the parents and the children!  If parents are too busy then maybe a reality check is in order i.e. a phone call to Children &amp; Youth Services would help them wake up.  <br /><br />As far as condoning welfare mothers popping out babies, I&#039;m the one that gets to see these kids first hand, especially when their parents are to damned high or drunk and these kids run the streets without the love, care or nurtuing of GOOD parents.  SO PLEASE  DON&#039;T EVEN GO THERE! These children are the victims and don&#039;t ever think for one minute I would stand up for such parents.  Matter of fact, I think the welfare system is backwards...instead of &quot;rewarding&quot; mothers for having more children by paying them more, they should be rewarding the mothers who are consciously making an effort to become independent and STOP having children we ultimately end up paying for!<br /><br />There is nothing I REPEAT nothing wrong with asking your older children to babysit occasionally provided there is a good reason and they are responsible enough to be trusted.<br /><br />What was the church meeting about???? Was it marriage counseling?  Geeez, I wouldn&#039;t want my children to know that mom and dad may have problems that they are trying to work out for the good of the family.  Why would I want to scare my children when such a devasting situation might never take place?  OR what if the older brother has problems that need to be work out through counseling?  Either way, it&#039;s none of our business.  The only thing that IS our business is trying to help Naithien, not to diss his parents.<br /><br />Agreed Naithien has his problems with his parents but ONLY through communication does this resolve itself.  You only see it through a child&#039;s eyes as justifiably you are still quite young.  I&#039;ve seen it both ways and let me tell you, you probably won&#039;t understand it all until you&#039;ve walked a few miles in my shoes.<br /><br />See how you feel 20+ years from now, when you have a family.  Until then, please don&#039;t go twisting my words and assuming that you have all the answers.  Parenting is not as easy as it looks.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td colspan="3" class="windowbg2" align="right">
							</td>
