						<tr class="titlebg">
							<td align="left" style="padding: 0 1ex;">
								8
							</td>
							<td width="75%" align="left">
								&nbsp;<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php#1">General Category</a> / <a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?board=2.0">Non-EQ (Hey Real Life!)</a> / <a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1273.15#msg12688">Re: Sibling Punishment. . .?</a>
							</td>
							<td align="right" style="padding: 0 1ex; white-space: nowrap;">
								on: November 16, 2004, 08:17:45 am
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left">My parents let me begin babysitting for a neighbor at age 14 with my mother only a few doors down (if I needed help).&nbsp; When she realized that I could handle it, she and my father agreed that on very special occasions, i.e. weddings (adult only), wedding anniversaries, etc.&nbsp; I could be trusted to watch my sisters, provided I follow the guidelines set down which my sisters were also aware of.&nbsp; I never betrayed their trust and my sisters always &quot;knew the score&quot;.&nbsp; &nbsp;Up until my dad&#039;s death, it was a written rule that I was in charge; however, mom&#039;s &quot;new&quot; improved system worked so well that this is the way I&#039;ve always handled babysitting with my own children.&nbsp; This way it&#039;s a democracy...each one feels important and no one feels as if they are considered the &quot;baby&quot;.&nbsp; <br /><br />Granted, times and situations change, but more so now than ever, it&#039;s extremely hard to find a good, trustworthy babysitter and what better way to show your children that they&#039;ve earned your trust and respect by allowing them to stay at home alone when the situation necessitates?</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td colspan="3" class="windowbg2" align="right">
							</td>
						</tr>
					</table>
				</td>
			</tr>
