						<tr>
							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left">Hmmm you stated that your brother was upset because his sister took his spot on the recliner.  Why did he leave the recliner?  Was she the instigator?  How old is she?  Sometimes we see the victim as just that but there are two sides to every story.  If he left the recliner for a legitimate reason (went to the bathroom and was coming right back) and came back to find your sister took his spot out of spite, then he may have been frustrated and just didn&#039;t use an appropriate way of handling the situation.  <br /><br />I would have had a talk with both of them, pointing out why it was wrong for her to take his seat and also explained why it was wrong for him not to have come to you with his problem.<br /><br />You were correct in disciplining him for his actions and I hope you explained this to him.  He hurt his sister and lied but at the same time; your brother felt he was wronged and no one saw his point.  He felt as if he was punished unjustly, while she was allowed to &quot;get away with it&quot; and that&#039;s why he threw a temper tantrum.  He felt he was owed an apology and I would have also have had both apologize to each other or if she&#039;s too young to apologize a &quot;hug to make up&quot; prior to refusing to give him his before bed drink, etc.<br /><br />All in all, you did a good job and acted maturely.  The punishment was in line for what your brother did; however the trick is understanding why he did it and then taking appropriate measures accordingly.<br /><br />It&#039;s all about respect...and you, as the big brother are in the best position to show this to your siblings, as they will &quot;take their lead&quot; by learning from your example.  Good Luck...it&#039;s not as easy as it looks.</td>
